{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi Ms. ___. I’m Dr. ___. I’m one of the medicine doctors. I heard you came in after dialysis feeling very weak and lightheaded. How are you feeling right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:21:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still kind of dizzy. Not like the room is spinning anymore, but I feel washed out. I couldn’t even get off my couch at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:22:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds scary. Any chest pain, shortness of breath, fever, belly pain, or diarrhea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:22:55",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No. I threw up once, but no diarrhea. No fever that I know of."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. I also see your blood pressure was low when EMS brought you in. After dialysis, do you usually run low?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:24:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, after dialysis I’m usually in the 80s or 90s on top. They tell me that’s my normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. One thing that stands out is you started pregabalin—Lyrica—two days ago for nerve pain. Is that right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:26:35",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. They gave it to me because my face pain is bad. But I felt off-balance on gabapentin before, so I was nervous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important. Lyrica can cause dizziness and weakness, and if you had a similar reaction to gabapentin, it makes it more likely. We’re going to hold the Lyrica now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:29:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So you think it’s the medicine and not an infection?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:30:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right now, medication side effect is high on the list. But because your immune system is very weak and your blood pressure was lower than usual, we’re also being cautious about infection. In the ER you got fluids and antibiotics to cover that possibility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:31:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "They told me I was on oxygen too. Am I okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re okay now. You’re breathing well on room air. We also did a chest X-ray and it didn’t show pneumonia or anything acute."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:34:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My head hurts too, but it always hurts on the right side since the shingles."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:35:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see that in your history—right V1 post-herpetic neuralgia and headaches. Any change from your usual headache today? New neck stiffness or confusion?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:36:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, it’s the same pain. I’m just tired and scared because I couldn’t move."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We’ll keep checking your vitals and labs. Your initial blood work shows a low white blood cell count, which we’ve seen before, and a mildly elevated lactate that improved after fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:39:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Does that mean I’m septic?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:40:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not necessarily. Lactate can go up when you’re dehydrated or your blood pressure is low. You don’t have fever or a clear source, and your exam doesn’t point to one. We sent blood cultures to be safe, and we’ll watch closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:42:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Are you going to keep giving me antibiotics?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:43:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "For tonight, we’ll continue broad coverage while we make sure your blood pressure stays stable and you don’t develop new symptoms. If things stay reassuring and cultures don’t grow anything, we can stop them."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I don’t make urine, so is it safe to give me fluids?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2131-09-29 17:46:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good question. We’re giving small, careful amounts and watching your lungs and oxygen. So far you’re breathing comfortably and your exam doesn’t show fluid overload."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2131-09-29 18:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Quick update: your blood pressure has come up after fluids. How’s the dizziness now when you sit up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2131-09-29 18:10:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better than before. Still weak though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2131-09-29 18:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll also check orthostatic vitals—blood pressure lying, sitting, standing—to see if it drops when you stand. For now, please call before getting up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2131-09-29 20:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Doctor, my skin is itching again and I keep scratching. Is that infection?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2131-09-29 20:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see the excoriations and the ulcer on your forehead where you’ve been picking. It doesn’t look hot or spreading like a skin infection right now. We’ll continue your topical creams and you can use hydroxyzine as needed for itching."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2131-09-29 20:07:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I still use the mupirocin on the rash?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2131-09-29 20:08:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—use it as prescribed on the areas you were told to treat. And try to avoid picking; it makes it harder to heal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Overnight you stayed afebrile and your blood pressure held steady. How are you feeling today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:15:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Less dizzy. I’m still tired. I’m worried it’ll happen again when I go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a fair worry. The strongest trigger we see is the Lyrica. Since we stopped it, you’re improving. Your orthostatic vitals were negative, meaning your pressure didn’t drop significantly when you stood."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:18:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I should never take Lyrica again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:19:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Correct—do not restart it. We’ll list it as a medication you don’t tolerate, especially given your prior balance problems on gabapentin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:20:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But then what do I do for the nerve pain in my face? It’s ruining my life."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We talked with your outpatient team and we’d like to try a different nerve-pain medicine: duloxetine, at a low dose, three times a week after dialysis. We’ll monitor for side effects like nausea or dizziness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:23:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that safe with my kidneys?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:24:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s why we’re using a low dose and only after dialysis three times a week. We’ll also ask you to stop it and call your doctor if you feel the same kind of severe dizziness or weakness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Am I still on antibiotics?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2131-09-30 08:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given you’ve stayed stable with no signs of infection, we’re stopping the antibiotics today. We’ll keep watching your vitals and we’ll follow the blood cultures until they finalize."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2131-09-30 10:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m confused about my HIV meds. I got blister packs but I haven’t started them."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2131-09-30 10:41:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for telling me. Right now in the hospital we’re continuing your prophylaxis medicines—Bactrim three times a week and azithromycin once a week—to prevent infections. For restarting your HIV treatment, we want you to coordinate with Dr. ___ right after discharge so you start the blister packs the right way."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2131-09-30 10:42:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Why can’t you just start them here?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2131-09-30 10:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We want to make sure the exact regimen in the blister packs matches what your HIV doctor intends and that you have the teaching and support set up. The most important thing is starting correctly and sticking with it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2131-09-30 14:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Dialysis is planned during this admission as scheduled. Any new symptoms—shortness of breath, chest pain, or palpitations?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2131-09-30 14:10:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No chest pain. Sometimes my heart races, but not right now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2131-09-30 14:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You have a history of atrial fibrillation, so if you feel racing heart, dizziness, or chest discomfort, tell us right away. Right now your rhythm looks regular."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2131-09-30 18:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Someone mentioned my mood. I’ve been feeling down, but I’m not trying to hurt myself."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2131-09-30 18:31:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I’m glad you told me. We take that seriously. We can have the team check in, and we strongly recommend outpatient psychiatry follow-up. If you ever have thoughts of harming yourself, you should seek help immediately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood cultures have not grown anything so far, and you’ve had no fevers. How’s your lightheadedness today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:05:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. I can sit up without feeling like I’ll pass out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. We think this was most consistent with a side effect from Lyrica plus low blood pressure after dialysis. We’re planning discharge today with home services."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:08:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my labs? They said my blood count was low."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:09:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your hemoglobin is lower than on admission, which can happen in kidney disease and with frequent blood draws. We’ll make sure your dialysis team and outpatient doctors keep monitoring it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my potassium? I heard it was high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:12:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your potassium was elevated on the morning labs, which is something we watch closely in ESRD. Dialysis is the main way we control that, and you’ll continue your regular dialysis schedule."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can you go over my medicines like I’m five? I get mixed up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. The big changes: stop Lyrica completely. Start duloxetine 20 mg—only three times a week, after dialysis. Keep taking Bactrim three times a week and azithromycin once a week to prevent infections. Continue sevelamer with meals and cinacalcet daily for your kidney-related minerals, and aspirin daily. Use acetaminophen as needed for pain, and hydroxyzine as needed for itching."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:17:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The duloxetine—only after dialysis, not every day?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:18:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Correct—only after dialysis, three times a week. If you take it daily by mistake, you could get more side effects."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:19:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What should make me come back to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2131-10-01 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Come back or call 911 for severe dizziness or fainting, chest pain, trouble breathing, new fever or chills, confusion, or if you can’t get up or keep fluids down. Also call if duloxetine makes you feel like Lyrica did."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2131-10-01 11:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will someone help me at home? I use a cane and sometimes a walker."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2131-10-01 11:31:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re arranging discharge home with services. And we want you to follow up with your outpatient social worker as well, since getting to appointments and managing meds has been hard."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2131-10-01 14:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Before you go, one more check: any dizziness when you stand now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2131-10-01 14:50:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I’m okay. Just my usual face pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2131-10-01 14:52:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "For the face pain, we’ll try the duloxetine and acetaminophen as needed. Keep your follow-up with your outpatient doctors so they can adjust the plan safely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2131-10-01 14:53:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the HIV meds—I should call Dr. ___ right away?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 70,
      "time": "2131-10-01 14:54:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. Please contact Dr. ___ to restart your HIV therapy using the blister packs, and ask about meeting with a pharmacist for medication teaching. Staying on treatment will help your immune system recover."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 71,
      "time": "2131-10-01 17:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your discharge paperwork is ready. Final recap: you came in with lightheadedness and weakness after dialysis, most likely from Lyrica. We stopped it, you improved, and there’s no evidence of pneumonia or bloodstream infection. Do you have any last questions before you head home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 72,
      "time": "2131-10-01 17:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, just… thank you. I’ll stay away from Lyrica and I’ll call my HIV doctor."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2131-09-29 17:10:00",
    "admission_end": "2131-10-01 17:33:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with advanced AIDS (CD4 22, high viral load, not yet restarted on antiretroviral therapy), ESRD from HIVAN on thrice-weekly hemodialysis via tunneled femoral catheter, chronic right V1 post-herpetic neuralgia/headache, eosinophilia, and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation was admitted after an episode of profound weakness and lightheadedness with one episode of non-bloody emesis shortly after dialysis, accompanied by hypotension. Given severe immunocompromise and low blood pressures, she initially received IV fluids and empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics; chest X-ray showed no acute process, lactate improved with fluids, and she remained afebrile without localizing infectious symptoms, with blood cultures showing no growth by discharge, so antibiotics were stopped. Her symptoms improved after discontinuation of recently started pregabalin (Lyrica), felt to be the most likely cause. She continued scheduled hemodialysis and opportunistic infection prophylaxis (TMP-SMX and azithromycin), received medication reconciliation and counseling due to low health literacy, and was started on low-dose duloxetine three times weekly after dialysis for neuropathic pain. She was discharged home with services with instructions to avoid pregabalin, monitor for recurrent dizziness, and follow closely with her HIV and dialysis teams, including support for restarting antiretroviral therapy using blister packs and outpatient psychiatry follow-up for depressed mood.",
    "problems": [
      "Dizziness and giddiness",
      "Unspecified adverse effect of other drug, medicinal and biological substance",
      "End stage renal disease",
      "Asymptomatic human immunodeficiency virus [HIV] infection status",
      "Herpes zoster with other nervous system complications",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Atrial fibrillation",
      "Anemia, unspecified",
      "Eosinophilia"
    ]
  }
}
